@smunini I envision the infrastructure as an Organization with multiple hospitals and multiple Oauth clients(SPA, mobile/desktop App's). Then we need an IDP server for authentication, BFF server implementing BFF design for Mobile and  DPoP and device bound tokens for mobile clients, such that security is done.  The OAuth server  issues an access token (FHIR spec does mention this) and the FHIR server decodes the access token to get the tenant context. So, the tenant context in a multitenancy environment should look like this - 
``` rust
pub struct TenantContext {
    /// Organization ID (tenant identifier)
    pub org_id: Uuid,
    /// Hospital ID within the organization
    pub hospital_id: Uuid,
    /// Authenticated user ID
    pub user_id: Option<Uuid>,
    /// Client ID (OAuth client that made the request)
    pub client_id: Option<String>,
    /// Scopes granted to this request
    pub scopes: Vec<String>,
    /// Roles associated with the user/client
    pub roles: Vec<String>,
}
```

Thank you Lukas for these comments and insights - much appreciated!

> I see that query_builder() takes a resource type parameter, however if I recall correctly, searches across a multitude of resources are allowed by the spec.

You are absolutely correct.  Good catch.  FHIR allows several forms of cross-resource-type searches:
1. System-level search (all resource types)
```
GET [base]?_id=123
GET [base]?_lastUpdated=gt2024-01-01
```
2. Explicit multi-type search using _type
```
GET [base]?_type=Condition,Observation&patient=Patient/123
```


So the SearchBackend trait needs revision. Instead of:
```rust
pub trait SearchBackend: Send + Sync {
    type QueryBuilder;
    type QueryResult;
    
    fn query_builder(&self, resource_type: &str) -> Self::QueryBuilder;
    // ...
}
```
It should probably be:
```rust
pub trait SearchBackend: Send + Sync {
    type QueryBuilder;
    type QueryResult;
    
    /// Creates a query builder for one or more resource types.
    /// If `resource_types` is None, searches all resource types.
    /// If `resource_types` is Some with multiple types, parameters must be common across all.
    fn query_builder(&self, resource_types: Option<&[&str]>) -> Self::QueryBuilder;
    
    async fn execute(&self, query: Self::QueryBuilder) -> Result<Self::QueryResult, SearchError>;
}
```
Similarly, `SearchProvider::search` takes a single `resource_type: &str`, but I also have `search_system` which takes `Option<&[&str]>`. The naming could be clearer—perhaps:
```rust
#[async_trait]
pub trait SearchProvider: ResourceStorage {
    /// Searches a single resource type.
    async fn search(
        &self,
        tenant: &TenantContext,
        resource_type: &str,
        query: &SearchQuery,
    ) -> Result<SearchBundle, StorageError>;

    /// Searches multiple resource types (or all if None).
    /// Parameters must be common across all searched types.
    async fn search_multi(
        &self,
        tenant: &TenantContext,
        resource_types: Option<&[&str]>,
        query: &SearchQuery,
    ) -> Result<SearchBundle, StorageError>;
}
```

> I would appreciate to allow TenantId to be arbitrary arbitrary strings or byte sequences. We use e.g. a two-level tenant id namespace in our system, sth like organization_id/hospital_id.

Good point!  Looking back at the document, `TenantId` is used throughout but never actually defined.

For flexibility, we could define it as an opaque wrapper over a string that supports both simple IDs and hierarchical namespaces:
```rust
/// A tenant identifier. Opaque to the storage layer—interpretation
/// of structure (flat vs. hierarchical) is left to the application.
/// 
/// # Examples
/// 
/// Simple flat identifiers:
/// ```
/// let tenant = TenantId::new("acme-health");
/// ```
/// 
/// Hierarchical namespaces:
/// ```
/// let tenant = TenantId::new("organization_id/hospital_id");
/// let tenant = TenantId::new("region:us-east/org:12345/facility:main");
/// ```
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
pub struct TenantId(String);

impl TenantId {
    pub fn new(id: impl Into<String>) -> Self {
        Self(id.into())
    }
    
    /// The system tenant, used for shared resources (terminology, conformance).
    pub const fn system() -> Self {
        Self(String::new())  // Or a sentinel like "__system__"
    }
    
    pub fn as_str(&self) -> &str {
        &self.0
    }
    
    /// Returns true if this is the system tenant.
    pub fn is_system(&self) -> bool {
        self.0.is_empty()  // Or check for sentinel
    }
}

impl From<&str> for TenantId {
    fn from(s: &str) -> Self {
        Self::new(s)
    }
}

impl From<String> for TenantId {
    fn from(s: String) -> Self {
        Self(s)
    }
}
```

This approach:

1. **Keeps `TenantId` opaque at the storage layer** — the storage traits don't parse or interpret the structure
2. **Supports simple string IDs** — `TenantId::new("acme")` 
3. **Supports hierarchical namespaces** — `TenantId::new("org/hospital")` or any other convention
4. **Leaves interpretation to the application** — if an application wants to enforce or parse hierarchy, it can do so at a higher layer

The one thing to note is that **index design** might care about hierarchy. If you're using `organization_id/hospital_id`, you might want indexes that support prefix queries for "all hospitals in this organization." But that's an implementation detail for specific backends, not something the trait layer needs to mandate.

> How will you handle multi-tenancy from the REST side? (different FHIR base endpoints like example.com/fhir/organization_id/hospital_id/Patient is how we will probably build it in our system)

I was thinking exactly the same thing.

> The current design only addresses offset bases pagination, which has O(N) complexity when the offset increases and data drift issues (HAPI caches the whole result set and then paginates into that). I personally lean more towards cursors.

You're right - Offset-based pagination (`_offset`) has fundamental scaling problems. To retrieve page N, the database must skip (N-1) × page_size rows—O(N) complexity that degrades as users page deeper. Worse, if data changes between page fetches, rows shift position, causing duplicates or gaps in results.

Cursor-based pagination avoids both problems. Instead of "skip N rows," the server returns an opaque continuation token encoding the current position. The client passes this token to fetch the next page, and the database seeks directly to that position—O(1) regardless of depth.
```rust
/// Pagination strategy for search results.
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub enum Pagination {
    /// Offset-based (supported for compatibility, discouraged for large result sets).
    Offset { count: u32, offset: u32 },
    
    /// Cursor-based (preferred). The cursor is opaque to clients.
    Cursor { count: u32, cursor: Option<PageCursor> },
}

/// An opaque pagination cursor. Internal structure is backend-specific.
/// 
/// For Cassandra, this wraps the paging state.
/// For PostgreSQL, this might encode a keyset (e.g., last seen `_lastUpdated` + `_id`).
/// For Elasticsearch, this wraps a search_after value or scroll ID.
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct PageCursor(Vec<u8>);

impl PageCursor {
    pub fn new(data: impl Into<Vec<u8>>) -> Self {
        Self(data.into())
    }
    
    pub fn as_bytes(&self) -> &[u8] {
        &self.0
    }
    
    /// Encodes the cursor for inclusion in a URL (base64).
    pub fn encode(&self) -> String {
        use base64::{Engine, engine::general_purpose::URL_SAFE_NO_PAD};
        URL_SAFE_NO_PAD.encode(&self.0)
    }
    
    /// Decodes a cursor from a URL parameter.
    pub fn decode(s: &str) -> Result<Self, PaginationError> {
        use base64::{Engine, engine::general_purpose::URL_SAFE_NO_PAD};
        let bytes = URL_SAFE_NO_PAD.decode(s)
            .map_err(|_| PaginationError::InvalidCursor)?;
        Ok(Self(bytes))
    }
}

/// Search results with pagination support.
pub struct SearchBundle {
    pub matches: Vec<StoredResource>,
    pub included: Vec<StoredResource>,
    pub total: Option<u64>,
    /// Cursor for the next page, if more results exist.
    pub next_cursor: Option<PageCursor>,
}
```

The `PageCursor` is intentionally opaque—different backends encode position differently:

| Backend | Cursor Contents |
|---------|-----------------|
| **Cassandra** | Native paging state from the driver |
| **PostgreSQL** | Keyset values: `(_lastUpdated, _id)` of last row |
| **MongoDB** | `_id` of last document, or resume token |
| **Elasticsearch** | `search_after` values, or scroll ID for deep pagination |
| **S3/Parquet** | Continuation token from `ListObjectsV2` |

#### Keyset Pagination for SQL Backends

For PostgreSQL and other relational databases, keyset pagination (also called "seek method") provides efficient cursor-based paging:
```sql
-- First page
SELECT * FROM observation
WHERE tenant_id = $1
ORDER BY last_updated DESC, id ASC
LIMIT 100;

-- Subsequent pages (cursor contains last_updated and id of final row)
SELECT * FROM observation
WHERE tenant_id = $1
  AND (last_updated, id) < ($last_updated, $last_id)
ORDER BY last_updated DESC, id ASC
LIMIT 100;
```

This requires a stable sort order with a unique tiebreaker (typically `_id`). The cursor encodes the sort key values of the last row returned.

#### Impact on SearchProvider

The `SearchProvider` trait accommodates both pagination styles:
```rust
#[async_trait]
pub trait SearchProvider: ResourceStorage {
    async fn search(
        &self,
        tenant: &TenantContext,
        resource_type: &str,
        query: &SearchQuery,
    ) -> Result<SearchBundle, StorageError>;
}

/// Updated SearchQuery with flexible pagination.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Default)]
pub struct SearchQuery {
    pub parameters: Vec<SearchParameter>,
    pub sort: Vec<SortSpec>,
    pub pagination: Option<Pagination>,
    pub summary: Option<SummaryMode>,
    pub elements: Option<Vec<String>>,
    pub include: Vec<IncludeSpec>,
    pub revinclude: Vec<IncludeSpec>,
}
```

Backends that only support offset pagination can reject cursor-based requests with `UnsupportedFeature`. Backends that support cursors should accept offset requests for compatibility but may log warnings for large offsets.

#### FHIR Bundle Links

FHIR represents pagination through Bundle links. The cursor appears in the `next` link URL:
```json
{
  "resourceType": "Bundle",
  "type": "searchset",
  "link": [
    {
      "relation": "self",
      "url": "https://example.org/fhir/Observation?patient=123&_count=100"
    },
    {
      "relation": "next",
      "url": "https://example.org/fhir/Observation?patient=123&_count=100&_cursor=eyJsYXN0X3VwZGF0ZWQiOiIyMDI0LTAxLTE1VDEwOjMwOjAwWiIsImlkIjoiYWJjMTIzIn0"
    }
  ]
}
```

The `_cursor` parameter is not part of the FHIR specification—it's a server-specific extension. Clients should treat pagination URLs as opaque and simply follow the `next` link.  This approach looks workable as described in the [FHIR spec](https://fhir.hl7.org/fhir/http.html#paging).


> In the transaction design, it would be great to also support optimistic locking

Agreed.  The base `Transaction` trait uses pessimistic locking.  Operations acquire locks and hold them until commit. This is simple but can cause contention under high concurrency.

Here are some initial design ideas and some implications for the FHIR API.

Optimistic locking assumes conflicts are rare. Instead of holding locks, the transaction tracks the versions of resources it has read and verifies they haven't changed at commit time. If any resource was modified by another transaction, the commit fails and the client retries.
```rust
/// Locking strategy for a transaction.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, Default)]
pub enum LockingStrategy {
    /// Pessimistic: acquire locks on read, hold until commit.
    /// Guarantees success at commit time but may cause contention.
    #[default]
    Pessimistic,
    
    /// Optimistic: track read versions, verify at commit.
    /// Better concurrency but may fail at commit time.
    Optimistic,
}

#[async_trait]
pub trait TransactionProvider: ResourceStorage {
    /// Begins a transaction with the default (pessimistic) locking strategy.
    async fn begin_transaction(
        &self,
        tenant: &TenantContext,
    ) -> Result<Box<dyn Transaction>, StorageError> {
        self.begin_transaction_with_options(tenant, TransactionOptions::default()).await
    }
    
    /// Begins a transaction with explicit options.
    async fn begin_transaction_with_options(
        &self,
        tenant: &TenantContext,
        options: TransactionOptions,
    ) -> Result<Box<dyn Transaction>, StorageError>;
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, Default)]
pub struct TransactionOptions {
    pub locking: LockingStrategy,
    pub isolation: IsolationLevel,
    /// Timeout for acquiring locks (pessimistic) or total transaction duration.
    pub timeout: Option<std::time::Duration>,
}
```

#### Optimistic Transaction Behavior

With optimistic locking, the transaction tracks which resources were read and their versions:
```rust
#[async_trait]
pub trait Transaction: Send + Sync {
    fn tenant(&self) -> &TenantContext;
    
    /// Returns the locking strategy for this transaction.
    fn locking_strategy(&self) -> LockingStrategy;

    /// Creates a resource within this transaction.
    async fn create(&mut self, resource: &Value) -> Result<StoredResource, StorageError>;

    /// Reads a resource within this transaction.
    /// 
    /// For optimistic transactions, this records the version for 
    /// conflict detection at commit time.
    async fn read(
        &self,
        resource_type: &str,
        id: &str,
    ) -> Result<Option<StoredResource>, StorageError>;
    
    /// Reads a resource and explicitly tracks it for optimistic locking,
    /// even if you don't need the contents. Useful when you want to 
    /// ensure a resource hasn't changed without fetching it again.
    async fn track_version(
        &mut self,
        resource_type: &str,
        id: &str,
        expected_version: &str,
    ) -> Result<(), StorageError>;

    /// Updates a resource within this transaction.
    async fn update(&mut self, resource: &Value) -> Result<StoredResource, StorageError>;

    /// Deletes a resource within this transaction.
    async fn delete(&mut self, resource_type: &str, id: &str) -> Result<(), StorageError>;

    /// Commits all operations in this transaction atomically.
    /// 
    /// For optimistic transactions, this verifies that all tracked
    /// resources still have the versions that were read. Returns
    /// `StorageError::OptimisticLockFailure` if any version has changed.
    async fn commit(self: Box<Self>) -> Result<(), StorageError>;

    /// Rolls back all operations in this transaction.
    async fn rollback(self: Box<Self>) -> Result<(), StorageError>;
}
```

#### Error Handling for Optimistic Lock Failures

When an optimistic transaction fails at commit time, the client needs enough information to retry intelligently:
```rust
#[derive(Debug)]
pub enum StorageError {
    // ... other variants ...
    
    /// Optimistic lock failure—a tracked resource was modified.
    OptimisticLockFailure {
        /// Resources that changed since they were read.
        conflicts: Vec<ConflictInfo>,
    },
    
    /// Version conflict on a single resource update.
    VersionConflict {
        resource_type: String,
        id: String,
        expected_version: String,
        actual_version: String,
    },
}

#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct ConflictInfo {
    pub resource_type: String,
    pub id: String,
    pub read_version: String,
    pub current_version: String,
}
```

#### When to Use Each Strategy

| Scenario | Recommended Strategy |
|----------|---------------------|
| Short-lived transactions, high contention | Pessimistic |
| Long-lived transactions, rare conflicts | Optimistic |
| FHIR transaction bundles (typically small) | Pessimistic |
| User-facing "edit and save" workflows | Optimistic |
| Batch processing with known non-overlapping data | Either |
| Distributed/multi-region deployments | Optimistic (locks don't span regions well) |

Backends that don't support optimistic locking can reject requests with `UnsupportedFeature`. Most relational databases support both; Cassandra's lightweight transactions are inherently optimistic for example.

### Interaction with FHIR Conditional Operations

FHIR defines conditional create, update, and delete operations that use search criteria rather than explicit IDs. These interact with transaction locking in important ways.

#### Conditional Create (`If-None-Exist`)

Conditional create only creates a resource if no matching resource exists:
```
POST [base]/Patient
If-None-Exist: identifier=http://example.org/mrn|12345
```

With pessimistic locking, the transaction locks the search space to prevent a concurrent create from slipping in. With optimistic locking, the "no match" condition is verified at commit time—if another transaction created a matching resource, the commit fails.
```rust
#[async_trait]
pub trait Transaction: Send + Sync {
    // ... existing methods ...
    
    /// Conditional create: creates only if search returns no matches.
    /// 
    /// With optimistic locking, the "no matches" assertion is verified
    /// at commit time. Returns `ConditionalCreateConflict` if a matching
    /// resource now exists.
    async fn create_if_none_exist(
        &mut self,
        resource: &Value,
        search_params: &[SearchParameter],
    ) -> Result<ConditionalOutcome, StorageError>;
}

#[derive(Debug)]
pub enum ConditionalOutcome {
    /// Resource was created.
    Created(StoredResource),
    /// Resource already existed (for conditional create).
    Existed { id: String, version: String },
    /// Multiple matches found (error condition).
    MultipleMatches { count: usize },
}
```

#### Conditional Update

Conditional update finds a resource by search criteria and updates it:
```
PUT [base]/Patient?identifier=http://example.org/mrn|12345
```

The semantics depend on match count:
- **No matches**: Create the resource (if server supports "upsert")
- **One match**: Update that resource
- **Multiple matches**: Error (412 Precondition Failed)
```rust
#[async_trait]
pub trait Transaction: Send + Sync {
    // ... existing methods ...
    
    /// Conditional update: updates the resource matching search criteria.
    /// 
    /// With optimistic locking, both the search result stability and 
    /// the matched resource's version are verified at commit time.
    async fn update_conditional(
        &mut self,
        resource: &Value,
        search_params: &[SearchParameter],
    ) -> Result<ConditionalOutcome, StorageError>;
    
    /// Conditional update with explicit version match (combines conditional
    /// search with ETag-style version verification).
    async fn update_conditional_with_match(
        &mut self,
        resource: &Value,
        search_params: &[SearchParameter],
        expected_version: &str,
    ) -> Result<ConditionalOutcome, StorageError>;
}
```

#### Conditional Delete

Conditional delete removes resources matching search criteria:
```
DELETE [base]/Observation?patient=Patient/123&status=cancelled
```

This is particularly tricky with optimistic locking because the set of matched resources could change between search and commit.
```rust
#[async_trait]
pub trait Transaction: Send + Sync {
    // ... existing methods ...
    
    /// Conditional delete: deletes resources matching search criteria.
    /// 
    /// With optimistic locking, the exact set of matched resources is
    /// recorded and verified at commit time. If the set has changed
    /// (resources added, removed, or modified), the commit fails.
    async fn delete_conditional(
        &mut self,
        resource_type: &str,
        search_params: &[SearchParameter],
    ) -> Result<ConditionalDeleteOutcome, StorageError>;
}

#[derive(Debug)]
pub enum ConditionalDeleteOutcome {
    /// No resources matched (not an error).
    NoneMatched,
    /// Single resource deleted.
    Deleted { id: String },
    /// Multiple resources deleted (if server policy allows).
    DeletedMultiple { count: usize },
    /// Multiple matches but server policy requires single match.
    MultipleMatchesNotAllowed { count: usize },
}
